Let the feasible pricing set be \(X=
set{x_0,x_1}
\) with reduced costs \(ar c(x_0)=0\) and \(ar c(x_1)=-1\). A search inspecting only \(x_0\) may correctly report that no inspected column is improving, yet the uninspected column \(x_1\) is improving. Hence failure to find a negative-reduced-cost column is not a certificate whenever the search omits a feasible state, transition, or terminal path that is neither safely dominated nor covered by a valid lower bound. Certification is recovered only if unexplored columns are proved to have reduced cost at least zero.
